WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.325
Lesson 8.6.

00:00:02.325 --> 00:00:04.355
Psychology and discipline.

00:00:04.355 --> 00:00:06.706
By the end of this lesson, you'll be able

00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:10.007
to recognise the behaviours that turn good strategies into

00:00:10.007 --> 00:00:12.190
losses, and build rules that stop them.

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:14.780
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:16.465
First, the key ideas.

00:00:16.465 --> 00:00:19.180
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:20.705
Then, your checklist.

00:00:20.705 --> 00:00:22.867
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:28.325
The operator's defence is structure: written theses, kill rules,

00:00:28.325 --> 00:00:32.399
caps, a journal, cooling-off periods and scheduled reviews.

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:34.944
Willpower in the moment is unreliable.

00:00:34.944 --> 00:00:38.432
FOMO, Jumping into a farm because everyone's

00:00:38.432 --> 00:00:42.839
posting about it, 24-hour change-control wait (14.3).

00:00:42.839 --> 00:00:45.807
Loss aversion, Holding a broken position hoping

00:00:45.807 --> 00:00:47.910
to "get back to even", Kill rules

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:50.189
written at entry, then followed.

00:00:50.189 --> 00:00:52.422
Sunk cost, "I've spent so much gas,

00:00:52.422 --> 00:00:54.531
I can't exit now", Judge only the

00:00:54.531 --> 00:00:56.754
future: would you enter today?.

00:00:56.754 --> 00:01:00.746
Overconfidence, Raising size after a winning streak, Caps

00:01:00.746 --> 00:01:04.144
are percentages; size changes only at review.

00:01:04.144 --> 00:01:07.124
Revenge trading, Adding leverage to win back

00:01:07.124 --> 00:01:09.309
a loss, No new leverage within 7

00:01:09.309 --> 00:01:10.999
days of a loss event.

00:01:10.999 --> 00:01:14.017
Anchoring, Refusing to sell below a past price,

00:01:14.017 --> 00:01:16.973
Decisions based on current data and the thesis.

00:01:18.273 --> 00:01:20.788
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:20.788 --> 00:01:22.807
After a 20% loss on a farm,

00:01:22.807 --> 00:01:25.331
Jordan wants to put 3× leverage on

00:01:25.331 --> 00:01:27.938
the next idea "to make it back".

00:01:27.938 --> 00:01:30.695
The rulebook says: no new leverage within

00:01:30.695 --> 00:01:32.533
7 days of a loss event, and

00:01:32.533 --> 00:01:35.458
size only changes at the monthly review.

00:01:35.458 --> 00:01:38.784
Jordan journals the urge, waits, and at review decides

00:01:38.784 --> 00:01:41.748
the idea doesn't meet the thesis standard at all.

00:01:43.048 --> 00:01:44.508
Here's your checklist.

00:01:44.508 --> 00:01:46.043
Do it now, for real.

00:01:46.043 --> 00:01:50.678
First, Personal rulebook written (cooling-off, loss rules, sizing rules).

00:01:50.678 --> 00:01:54.573
Next, Emotions noted in the journal next to decisions.

00:01:54.573 --> 00:01:56.667
And finally, Monthly review includes

00:01:56.667 --> 00:01:58.138
"which rule did I break?".

00:01:59.438 --> 00:02:00.648
Question 1.

00:02:00.648 --> 00:02:02.223
What's the sunk-cost question?

00:02:06.643 --> 00:02:08.863
The answer: Would I enter this position

00:02:08.863 --> 00:02:10.583
today, knowing what I know now?

00:02:11.883 --> 00:02:13.128
Question 2.

00:02:13.128 --> 00:02:15.603
Why rely on structure rather than willpower?

00:02:20.023 --> 00:02:22.423
The answer: Decisions under stress are

00:02:22.423 --> 00:02:25.193
unreliable; pre-written rules decide for you.

00:02:26.493 --> 00:02:27.763
Question 3.

00:02:27.763 --> 00:02:29.503
Rule against revenge trading?

00:02:33.923 --> 00:02:36.649
The answer: For example, no new leverage

00:02:36.649 --> 00:02:38.643
within 7 days of a loss event.

00:02:39.943 --> 00:02:42.458
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:02:42.458 --> 00:02:45.802
Next up, Lesson 8.7: Quantitative risk:

00:02:45.802 --> 00:02:49.983
volatility, VaR, drawdown, correlation and sizing.
